Exercise 9.4.8 Common Terminal Commands 
Part I
1. make a directory called first
    mk dir first
2. change directory to the first folder
    cd first
3. create a file called person.txt
    touch person.txt
4. change the name of person.txt to another.txt
    mv person.txt another.txt
5. make a copy of the another.txt file and call it copy.txt
    cp another.txt copy.txt
6. remove the copy.txt file
    rm copy.txt
7. make a copy of the first folder and call it second
    cp -r first second (make sure you are in the proper directory)
8. delete the second folder
    rm -rf second

Part II
1. What does the man command do? Type in man rm. How do you scroll and get out?
    Man command will bring you to the manual. Scrolling is done by pressing D to advance half a page, B to go back one page, and U to go back half a page; Q is used to exit the manual. 
2. Look at the man page for ls. What does the -l flag do? What does the -a flag do?
    -l Flag uses a long listing format, meaning a more detailed response. 
    -a Flag lists all files, including hidden ones. 
3. How do you jump between words in the terminal?
    Jumping between words in the terminal can be done by using option left/right.
4. How do you get to the end of a line in terminal?
    Use option+e to move to the end of a line in terminal.
5. How do you move your cursor to the beginning in terminal?
    Use option+a to move the cursor to the beginning of the terminal line. 
6. How do you delete a word (without pressing backspace multiple times) in terminal?
    Deleting a complete word in the terminal is done with option+delete. This saves you from pressing backspace the correct number of times for each character.
7. What is the difference between a terminal and shell?
    The terminal is an interface program that runs within the shell. The shell is what handles commands whereas the terminal allows you to interact with the environment. 
8. What is an absolute path?
    An absolute path starts at the root route and gives the complete location for a file or folder. 
9. What is an relative path?
    A relative path also gives the path showing where a file or folder is located, yet it starts at the current location and not the root route. 
10. What is a flag? Give three examples of flags you have used.
    Flags are additional functionality given to existing commands to give further and often more detailed instruction for the commands. For instance, the -a flag will show all files, including hidden ones when using the ls command.
11. What do the r and f flags do with the rm command?
    -r remove folders and recursively go through each folder and file.
    -f will force removal.
